# [Create a chat completion]($heading.id('create-a-chat-completion'))

Send JSON to `POST /v1/chat/completions` with the ID returned by
[Models]($link.page('llmd/api-reference/models').ref('read-the-model-id')).
The Basic and Streaming examples use `LiquidAI/LFM3-1.7B-FP8`.

## [Read the response]($heading.id('read-the-response'))

```=html
<div class="chat-example-pair">
  <div class="command-shell">
    <span class="command-language">Request · Shell</span>
    <pre><code class="sh" data-copy-label="Copy basic chat request">curl -fsS http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1/chat/completions \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{
    "model": "LFM3-1.7B-FP8",
    "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Reply with one short greeting."}],
    "max_completion_tokens": 64
  }'</code></pre>
  </div>
  <div class="command-shell">
    <span class="command-language">Illustrative response · JSON</span>
    <pre><code class="json">{
  "model": "LFM3-1.7B-FP8",
  "choices": [{
    "index": 0,
    "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Hello!"},
    "finish_reason": "stop"
  }],
  "usage": {
    "prompt_tokens": 12,
    "completion_tokens": 2,
    "total_tokens": 14
  }
}</code></pre>
  </div>
</div>
```

Read `choices[0].message.content` for the complete reply and
`choices[0].finish_reason` for the reason generation ended. The generated text
and token counts vary.

# [Stream a chat completion]($heading.id('stream-a-chat-completion'))

Set `stream` to `true`. With curl, `-N` displays events as they arrive.

```=html
<div class="chat-example-pair">
  <div class="command-shell">
    <span class="command-language">Request · Shell</span>
    <pre><code class="sh" data-copy-label="Copy streaming chat request">curl -fsSN http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1/chat/completions \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{
    "model": "LFM3-1.7B-FP8",
    "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Reply with one short greeting."}],
    "max_completion_tokens": 64,
    "stream": true,
    "stream_options": {"include_usage": true}
  }'</code></pre>
  </div>
  <div class="command-shell">
    <span class="command-language">Illustrative response · SSE</span>
    <pre><code class="text">data: {"choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"Hello"}}]}

data: {"choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"!"},"finish_reason":"stop"}]}

data: {"choices":[],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":12,"completion_tokens":2,"total_tokens":14}}

data: [DONE]</code></pre>
  </div>
</div>
```

The response uses `text/event-stream`. Check for `data: [DONE]` before parsing
the event data as JSON. Append `choices[0].delta.content` when present. The
requested usage event has an empty `choices` array. For streaming tool calls,
assemble the `delta.tool_calls` fragments by tool-call index before parsing
the arguments.

# [Call tools from a client]($heading.id('call-tools-from-a-client'))

Replace `TOOL_CAPABLE_MODEL_ID` in both Tools commands with the served ID of a
model and chat template that support tool calling. The first request offers
`get_local_time` and asks for the time in Paris.

```=html
<div class="chat-example-pair">
  <div class="command-shell">
    <span class="command-language">Request · Shell</span>
    <pre><code class="sh" data-copy-label="Copy tool call request">curl -fsS http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1/chat/completions \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{
    "model": "TOOL_CAPABLE_MODEL_ID",
    "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "What time is it in Paris?"}],
    "tools": [{
      "type": "function",
      "function": {
        "name": "get_local_time",
        "description": "Return the local time in a city",
        "parameters": {
          "type": "object",
          "properties": {"city": {"type": "string"}},
          "required": ["city"]
        }
      }
    }]
  }'</code></pre>
  </div>
  <div class="command-shell">
    <span class="command-language">Illustrative response · JSON</span>
    <pre><code class="json">{
  "choices": [{
    "index": 0,
    "message": {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "",
      "tool_calls": [{
        "id": "0d4c9f2a7b3e6018",
        "type": "function",
        "function": {
          "name": "get_local_time",
          "arguments": "{\"city\":\"Paris\"}"
        }
      }]
    },
    "finish_reason": "tool_calls"
  }]
}</code></pre>
  </div>
</div>
```

When the model requests a call, read `choices[0].message.tool_calls`. Each
function call carries an `id`, a `function.name`, and `function.arguments`
encoded as a JSON string. The client validates and executes the function.

```=html
<details class="client-disclosure">
  <summary>Show the follow-up request</summary>
  <p>Keep the original user and assistant messages in order. Append a message with <code>role</code> set to <code>tool</code>, <code>content</code> set to your function result, and <code>tool_call_id</code> set to the ID from the assistant’s call. The example uses <code>0d4c9f2a7b3e6018</code> in both places. Use the actual assistant message, call ID, and result from your run.</p>
  <div class="chat-example-pair">
    <div class="command-shell">
      <span class="command-language">Follow-up request · Shell</span>
      <pre><code class="sh" data-copy-label="Copy tool follow-up request">curl -fsS http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1/chat/completions \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{
    "model": "TOOL_CAPABLE_MODEL_ID",
    "messages": [
      {"role": "user", "content": "What time is it in Paris?"},
      {
        "role": "assistant",
        "content": "",
        "tool_calls": [{
          "id": "0d4c9f2a7b3e6018",
          "type": "function",
          "function": {
            "name": "get_local_time",
            "arguments": "{\"city\":\"Paris\"}"
          }
        }]
      },
      {
        "role": "tool",
        "tool_call_id": "0d4c9f2a7b3e6018",
        "content": "14:30"
      }
    ]
  }'</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="command-shell">
      <span class="command-language">Illustrative response · JSON</span>
      <pre><code class="json">{
  "choices": [{
    "index": 0,
    "message": {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The local time in Paris is 14:30."
    },
    "finish_reason": "stop"
  }]
}</code></pre>
    </div>
  </div>
</details>
```

# [Request fields]($heading.id('request-fields'))

| Request field | Type and default | Behavior |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `model` | String, required | ID from `GET /v1/models`. |
| `messages` | Array, required | Conversation history in order. A text message has a `role` and `content`. The loaded model's chat template determines supported roles and content types. |
| `max_completion_tokens` | Integer, optional | Limit generated tokens. The server also enforces its context limit. The older `max_tokens` field is used when `max_completion_tokens` is absent. |
| `stream` | Boolean, `false` | Set to `true` for server-sent events. |
| `stream_options.include_usage` | Boolean, `false` | Emit an additional usage event when streaming. |
| `temperature` | Number, optional | Request-level sampling temperature. |
| `top_p` | Number, optional | Request-level nucleus sampling threshold. Use `0` through `1`. |
| `tools` | Array, optional | Offer function definitions to a tool-capable model and chat template. |

For the request-level sampling behavior documented in
[LLMD 20261112.0](https://zml.ai/posts/llmd-20261112.0/#sampling-controls),
`temperature` and `top_p` override the CLI value, then the model recommendation
and fallback apply when needed. See [Sampling and decoding]($link.page('llmd/cli-reference/sampling-and-decoding'))
for server defaults. Additional request fields and modalities depend on the
release and model. Verify that optional fields have the intended effect on
your release because the server can ignore unknown JSON fields.

# [Errors]($heading.id('errors'))

| Result | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `400 Bad Request` | Check required fields, JSON types, and the model's supported content types. |
| `400` with `error.code` set to `context_length_exceeded` | The tokenized prompt reached the server context cap. Shorten the conversation or review the server configuration. |
